WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.322 --> 00:00:06.198
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.198 --> 00:00:08.729
Do you think you're tough?

00:00:08.729 --> 00:00:10.540
How tough?

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:12.540
How much adversity can you overcome?

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.480
I want to give you
a couple of scenarios and

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.860
I want you to take a second,
put yourself in each one.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:21.880
What would you do if your boss
gave you a negative review?

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.490
What would you if you pitched your
startup, and the investor passed?

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.340
What would you do if the company you
spent 20 years building went bankrupt?

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:36.370
What would you do if you
were diagnosed with cancer?

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.570
Now this guy in front is thinking,
whoa whoa, that first set of questions

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:44.760
is completely different from
that second set of questions.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.140
A bad review is nothing like cancer.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:52.406
And I would say, exactly,
that's the point of my talk.

00:00:52.406 --> 00:00:55.630
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I want to show that not only are there

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:59.210
different kinds of adversity, there
are also different kinds of resilience.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:03.040
There are lots of words for
this characteristic.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.050
Resilience, emotional fortitude,
toughness, grit.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:08.740
They have slightly different meanings.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:10.270
They overlap some, but by and

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:14.070
large, they refer to the ability of
individuals to overcome adversity.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:17.770
The problem is that no
matter what words we use,

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:19.630
we tend to think of it
as a single spectrum.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.740
Really weak people get
overwhelmed by one bad review.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:26.800
Really tough people can watch their
whole career go up in smoke, and

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:27.380
not miss a step.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:29.870
But that doesn't capture all of it.

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:35.400
As the guy in front pointed out,
these are different kinds of stressors.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.470
It would make sense that they would
illicit different reactions, and

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:42.270
require different coping mechanisms,
even from a tough person.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:46.330
This has been bothering me and I had
a sudden burst of clarity when I realized

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:50.560
that these same words that we used to
describe people, also have meanings when

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.780
they describe the ways that physical
materials deform under stress.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.660
In mechanical engineering, deformation and

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.550
reaction to stress follows
an established pattern.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:03.160
Materials first deform elastically,

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:06.440
which is to say that they
bend in response to stress.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.290
But when the stress is removed,
they bend back.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:12.760
This period of elastic deformation
continues to a point called

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:13.360
the yield strength.

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:15.598
Here's some calculus, you ready?

00:02:15.598 --> 00:02:18.890
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The area under the curve between

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:22.370
the origin and yield strength
is actually called toughness.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:27.510
Toughness is effectively a measure of
how much stress the material can take

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:28.580
and still recover.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.420
It dovetails remarkably well with
definitions that we see in psychology.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.250
In mechanical engineering,
there's a little bit more.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.580
What happens when stress
exceeds yield strength?

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.860
Mechanically, it's called
plastic deformation.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:43.840
The material bends permanently or

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:49.330
even breaks at what's known as a fracture
point, which leads to what happened to me.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:55.830
I fractured my tibial plateau,
where the shin bone meets the knee joint,

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:57.890
I was tobogganing and
I lost control, hit a pole.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.410
To mitigate the risks of internal
bleeding, I needed surgery that night.

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:05.060
Surgeons drilled two metal
rods into my tibia, and

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:09.280
two metal rods into my femur, connected
all four with an external fixation device.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:13.330
A week later, I had another surgery in
which they implanted a metal rod and

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:14.080
a dozen screws.

00:03:15.412 --> 00:03:21.440
All told, the sentence was 16 days in
the hospital, 12 weeks on crutches,

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.910
a year of physical therapy,
and unknown permanent damage.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:28.810
I hit my fracture point, literally.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:31.380
How does that happen?

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.040
It's different for everyone, but

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.734
I'd say we go there when something really
important to us changes permanently.

00:03:39.734 --> 00:03:45.790
A job, a reputation, a relationship,
or in my case, a leg.

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:50.810
That idea of permanent damage to
the leg that I had intended to use for

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:54.780
marathon running and mountain climbing,
the leg that I was maybe a little vainly

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:58.990
proud of, hit me hard,
harder than other challenges I'd overcome.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:04.080
Somehow, I couldn't help but think,
what if this weren't my leg?

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.190
How would I react if this were my company?

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:10.230
That was a scary thought.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.230
I'd long nurtured
entrepreneurial inclinations and

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:14.340
the market can dish out plenty
of challenges of its own.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.760
If I was flat on my
back with a broken leg,

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:21.947
how could I reasonably expect to lead in
the face of corporate-sized adversity?

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:30.490
This is where mechanical engineering,
of all things, made so much sense to me.

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:33.090
Mechanically, there are two types
of deformation reactions in

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:34.550
response to stress.

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:38.300
In elastic deformation, materials bend but
they end up back where they were,

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:42.400
in plastic deformation they bend or
break and need to be rebuilt.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.528
I think that people bend two different
ways as well and that means there should

00:04:46.528 --> 00:04:51.080
be two different such a skills for coping
with stress, but we mostly talk about one.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:57.220
We call people resilient when they can
take a punch and get right back up.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:01.590
Being tough is certainly a good goal,
it's been one of mine for a long time.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:03.710
But what about being good at rebuilding?

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:08.070
What do you do after,
not just a punch but a knockout?

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.770
That was something I hadn't been taught,
and so

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:14.870
I went about trying to
find best practices.

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:18.620
My favorites came from
the field of survivorship,

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:21.200
research on people who have made it
out of life and death situations.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:24.990
The first thing successful
survivors do right

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:27.420
is move quickly from denial to acceptance.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:30.750
They don't dwell on what's been lost or

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:32.548
think about the unfairness
of their predicament.

00:05:32.548 --> 00:05:37.410
Even in non-life threatening situations,
that's so much easier said than done.

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:39.650
But it can be done.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:45.550
When Jimmy Carter lost the election in
1980, he said that he went home, slept for

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:51.370
an entire day and awoke to what he called
a new, unwanted, potentially empty life.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:54.940
Unwanted, that's the tricky part.

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:57.170
But only potentially empty.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:01.020
Carter never became president again, but

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:05.350
he did move on to international conflict
mediation and the Nobel Peace Prize.

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:11.920
A second trait of successful survivors
is the ability to set small goals and

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:14.635
the willingness to celebrate small wins.

00:06:14.635 --> 00:06:18.050
Recognizing even small victories can help
prevent a descent into hopelessness.

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:22.920
In 2013, ski racer Lindsey Vonn
suffered a catastrophic crash.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.750
She tore her ACL, MCL, meniscus and
fractured her tibial plateau.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:32.590
I can't know whether she
did any wallowing but

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:36.960
I do know that two days after surgery,
she was doing core strength exercises.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:39.270
Three weeks after surgery,
she was on a bike.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:40.990
As soon as she could bear weight,
she did squats.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:47.550
She didn't actually ski for 203 days but
by the time she did, she was strong.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:52.340
Finally, survivors get help but
they also give it.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:55.390
Giving help takes you out of yourself.

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:57.680
It lets you be a rescuer, not a victim.

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:02.160
When Mickey Drexler was fired
from his role as CEO of the GAP,

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:05.618
he found another company to help,
in the form of a struggling J.Crew brand.

00:07:05.618 --> 00:07:08.960
He invested $10 million of his own money,

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.610
took one-tenth of his former salary, and
managed to catapult the company from a $30

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:17.720
million operating loss to a $37
million profit in just a year.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:23.330
Move on from what's lost,
set small goals, get and give help.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:28.390
Whether these things really make
the difference, I'm not sure.

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:29.740
I'm trying.

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:33.170
What I do know is that all of us,
at some point, either already have or

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:35.880
will need to rebuild and
I'm convinced that this

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:41.530
rebuilding requires different
skills than traditional resilience.

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:48.030
In the wake of trauma, almost everyone
experiences some depression anxiety.

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:51.190
Most go on to move past
those early feelings and

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:54.370
some become psychologically
stronger than they were before.

00:07:55.645 --> 00:07:58.480
Psychologists call it
Post Traumatic Growth.

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:00.310
It's what I'm hoping for
when I talk about rebuilding.

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:04.150
You guessed it, I have to pull up
my favorite graph one more time.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:10.120
In elastic deformation, you bend but
you end up exactly where you were.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:15.140
In plastic deformation, you bend or break
and you're going to end up different.

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:19.527
What kind of different
depends upon how you rebuild.

00:08:19.527 --> 00:08:23.289
Bouncing back and
rebuilding are different skill sets.

00:08:23.289 --> 00:08:24.543
Let's develop both.

00:08:24.543 --> 00:08:25.378
Thank you.

00:08:25.378 --> 00:08:28.142
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:28.142 --> 00:08:32.678
[MUSIC]

